# Plan-first workflow, version 2

Capabilities: `planner`, `implementer`, and `integrator` task roles; planning,
review, implementation, and integration phases; and a user-approved local landing
gate. Change requests, live testing, and release are not part of this workflow.

This is a deliberately small workflow for turning a task description into a plan
and then implementing that plan. Read WORKSPACE.md before every decision and use
workspace menu --json for the current phase and available actions.

## 1. Plan

Read inputs/issue.md and repository instructions. Create one planner Task with the
required PLAN.md artifact, one planning worktree at the frozen workspace base, and
one planner Agent. The planner analyzes the code and writes a bounded plan without
implementing the change. Review and accept the handoff, then advance to plan_review.

## 2. Prepare implementation

Turn the accepted plan into one or more implementation Tasks. Every implementation
task must depend on the accepted planner task and have clear acceptance criteria.
Create a separate worktree for each implementation task; never run overlapping
writers in the same worktree. Advance to implementing after the task graph is ready.

## 3. Implement

Start ready implementation Tasks in their worktrees. Workers commit their changes,
write IMPLEMENTATION.md and real check evidence, then submit a handoff. Review and
accept every successful implementation handoff. When all implementation tasks are
accepted, advance to integration.

## 4. Integrate and land

Prepare an integration worktree with `workspace integration prepare`. Create one
integrator Task that merges accepted implementation heads with `git merge --no-ff`,
resolves textual conflicts, runs project checks, records every resolution in
INTEGRATION.md, commits, and submits a handoff. If a conflict requires a product or
implementation change, ask the orchestrator a question and stop instead of guessing.

After the integrator handoff is accepted, review the result and run
`workspace integration land --expected-revision N --user-confirmed`. Landing is a
local fast-forward into the target branch and never pushes to a remote. Finally run
`workspace complete --expected-revision N --user-confirmed`.

On resume, inspect existing tasks, logical sessions, run history, worktrees, handoffs and artifacts before
creating anything. Use stable operation keys and retry only after preserving partial
work. Ask the user only about unresolved product choices or blockers.
